---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import StorySteps from '../../components/StorySteps.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/charts/Figure.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/charts/BarChart.astro';
import StatBand from '../../components/charts/StatBand.astro';

const steps = [
  {
    kicker: 'The situation',
    headline: 'A model to prioritise the leads worth chasing',
    body:
      'Counsellor time is the scarce resource, not leads. I built a model to predict which leads would convert, so the team could spend that time on the ones most likely to become clients.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'The red flag',
    headline: 'It scored 100% — which meant it was cheating',
    body:
      'A perfect score is not a win, it is a symptom. The model had access to information that only exists after a lead has already converted, so it was reading the answer off the back of the page. Ship that, and the team trusts something that collapses on the first real lead.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'What I did',
    headline: 'Found the leakage, deleted it, rebuilt honestly',
    body:
      'I audited every feature for whether it would actually be available at scoring time, removed the leaking fields, and retrained. The honest model lands at 77% accuracy against a 62% majority-class baseline — a real 15-point lift over guessing.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'The commercial result',
    headline: 'A defensible way to rank leads and channels',
    body:
      'The top-scoring 20% of leads convert at 84%, more than double the average. And the model surfaced something leadership could act on immediately: referral leads convert at 90% against 26% for paid social. That is a quality-over-volume argument with evidence behind it.',
  },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="The most valuable thing was what I threw away"
  eyebrow="Impact story · Modelling → Strategy"
  hook="My prediction model scored a suspicious 100% — and the smartest move was deleting the data that made it perfect."
  description="A lead conversion model that scored 100% was leaking. Audited, rebuilt honestly at 77% versus a 62% baseline, and turned into a commercial insight about lead quality."
  org="The Migration"
  year="2026"
  stack={['Python', 'scikit-learn', 'Pandas', 'Feature Leakage', 'Lead Scoring', 'Model Validation']}
>
  <p class="lead">
    A model that is never wrong has usually been told the answer. The interesting part
    of this project was not building it — it was working out why it was too good.
  </p>

  <StorySteps steps={steps} />

  <Figure
    title="Accuracy: the leaking model, the honest model, and the baseline"
    sub="Majority-class baseline is what you get by guessing 'will not convert' every time"
  >
    <BarChart
      rows={[
        { label: 'Leaking model', value: 100, display: '100%' },
        { label: 'Honest model', value: 77, display: '77%' },
        { label: 'Baseline (guess)', value: 62, display: '62%' },
      ]}
      max={100}
      highlight={['Honest model']}
      categoryHeader="Model"
      valueHeader="Accuracy"
    />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    The bar on top is the one to distrust. The only number that means anything here is
    the gap between the honest model and the baseline: <strong>77% against 62%</strong>.
    That gap is the actual value the model adds over doing nothing, and it is the number
    I would defend in a room.
  </p>

  <h2>What the model was reading</h2>
  <p>
    Leakage is rarely malicious and almost never obvious. It hides in fields that look
    innocuous until you ask <em>when does this value get written?</em> — the question
    that separates a feature from a label in disguise.
  </p>

  <div class="leak-table">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Why it leaked</th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Pipeline stage</td>
          <td>Only advances to the later stages once the lead has already converted.</td>
          <td><span class="verdict-out">Removed</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Payment / invoice fields</td>
          <td>Populated at the moment of conversion — the label wearing a different name.</td>
          <td><span class="verdict-out">Removed</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Total touchpoint count</td>
          <td>Counted the full lifetime of the record, including activity after conversion.</td>
          <td><span class="verdict-out">Rebuilt</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Source / channel</td>
          <td>Known the moment the lead arrives. Genuinely available at scoring time.</td>
          <td><span class="verdict-in">Kept</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>First-response time</td>
          <td>Known early in the lifecycle, before any conversion decision.</td>
          <td><span class="verdict-in">Kept</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The fix for "total touchpoints" is worth calling out, because deleting it would have
    thrown away real signal. Instead I rebuilt it as a point-in-time feature — touchpoints
    within the first 72 hours — which is knowable at scoring time and kept most of its
    predictive power.
  </p>

  <h2>From model to decision</h2>
  <p>
    An accuracy figure is not an insight. What made this useful to leadership was
    translating it into two things they could act on: which leads to work first, and
    which channels are worth the money.
  </p>

  <Figure
    title="Conversion rate by lead score decile"
    sub="Leads ranked by model score, split into ten equal groups"
    note="Illustrative deciles; the top-20% and overall-average figures are the measured ones."
  >
    <BarChart
      rows={[
        { label: 'Top 10%', value: 89, display: '89%' },
        { label: '10–20%', value: 79, display: '79%' },
        { label: '20–40%', value: 52, display: '52%' },
        { label: '40–60%', value: 33, display: '33%' },
        { label: '60–80%', value: 17, display: '17%' },
        { label: 'Bottom 20%', value: 6, display: '6%' },
      ]}
      max={100}
      highlight={['Top 10%', '10–20%']}
      categoryHeader="Score decile"
      valueHeader="Conversion rate"
    />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    The top two deciles — the highest-scoring 20% of leads — convert at
    <strong>84%</strong>, more than double the overall average. That is a concrete
    working instruction: these are the calls to make first thing in the morning.
  </p>

  <Figure
    title="Conversion rate by acquisition channel"
    sub="The same leads, cut by where they came from"
  >
    <BarChart
      rows={[
        { label: 'Referral', value: 90, display: '90%' },
        { label: 'Organic / SEO', value: 48, display: '48%' },
        { label: 'Paid social', value: 26, display: '26%' },
      ]}
      max={100}
      highlight={['Referral']}
      categoryHeader="Channel"
      valueHeader="Conversion rate"
    />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    Referral leads convert at <strong>90%</strong> against <strong>26%</strong> for paid
    social — a 3.5× difference in quality that a cost-per-lead figure completely hides.
    A cheap lead that never converts is not cheap. This gave the team a defensible reason
    to shift effort toward the sources that actually produce clients.
  </p>

  <StatBand
    stats={[
      { value: '77%', label: 'Honest accuracy (62% baseline)' },
      { value: '84%', label: 'Conversion in the top-scoring 20%' },
      { value: '3.5×', label: 'Referral vs paid social conversion' },
      { value: '0', label: 'Leaking features shipped' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Why I tell this story</h2>
  <p>
    It shows the two things I want a team to know about how I work. I am technically
    capable enough to build the thing — and I care more about a number being
    <em>true</em> than <em>impressive</em>. A 100% model would have looked better in a
    slide deck for exactly as long as it took someone to rely on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the output was not a model. It was a commercial insight about consumer
    behaviour — quality over volume, with the evidence to back it — which is where
    analytics actually earns its keep.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .leak-table { overflow-x: auto; margin-block: 1.8rem 2.2rem; }
  .leak-table table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .9rem; min-width: 34rem; }
  .leak-table th, .leak-table td { text-align: left; padding: .7rem .8rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); vertical-align: top; }
  .leak-table th { font-weight: 600; background: var(--surface-2); font-size: .82rem; }
  .verdict-out, .verdict-in {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: .7rem;
    padding: .18rem .5rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .verdict-out { background: color-mix(in srgb, #d03b3b 14%, transparent); color: #b03232; }
  .verdict-in { background: var(--accent-tint); color: var(--accent); }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .verdict-out { color: #f08a8a; }
  }
</style>
